---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  REPO_URL,
  REPO_BRANCH,
  reportUrl,
  newEntryUrl,
  correctionEmail,
  newEntryEmail,
  CONTACT_EMAIL,
} from '../site.config';

// Contributor wall (Phase K): names drawn from the Git history at build time.
// Guarded so a missing/unavailable git history never breaks the build.
let contributors: { name: string; count: number }[] = [];
try {
  const { execFileSync } = await import('node:child_process');
  const out = execFileSync('git', ['shortlog', '-sn', '--all', '--no-merges'], {
    encoding: 'utf8',
    stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'ignore'],
  });
  contributors = out
    .trim()
    .split('\n')
    .map((line) => {
      const m = line.trim().match(/^(\d+)\s+(.+)$/);
      return m ? { count: Number(m[1]), name: m[2] } : null;
    })
    .filter((c): c is { name: string; count: number } => c !== null);
} catch {
  contributors = [];
}
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — Mission Sikhism"
  description="A non-profit, community-built, fully-sourced history of the Sikh faith — and how to contribute to it."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container about">
      <span class="kicker">The project</span>
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Mission Sikhism is a non-profit, community-built history of the Sikh
        faith, from 1469 to today — offered freely, without paywalls, in the
        spirit of <em>sarbat da bhala</em>, the welfare of all.
      </p>

      <h2>Our one promise</h2>
      <p>
        Sikh history is often retold without references. This site takes the
        opposite approach: <strong>every factual claim is tied to a citation</strong>,
        and nothing is published until it is. No page here rests on memory or
        received wisdom alone. You can see the result on the
        <a href="/library">Library</a> page, which gathers every source cited
        across the whole site into one bibliography.
      </p>

      <h2>How the site is built</h2>
      <p>
        The history is organised three ways over one set of facts: as a
        <a href="/history">timeline</a>, as an interactive <a href="/map">map</a>
        where every event is placed where it happened, and as the
        <a href="/people">people</a> who shaped it. Alongside sit
        <a href="/faith">The Faith</a> and <a href="/culture">Culture &amp;
        Heritage</a>. It is a static, open project — plain text files in a public
        repository — so it is fast, durable, and free to run.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contribute">Contribute</h2>
      <p>
        This project grows as research grows, and it is better for more eyes. If
        you have spotted an inaccuracy, found a stronger source, or know of an
        event not yet documented, we would like to hear from you. You do not need
        to be a historian, and you do not need an account anywhere.
      </p>
      <p>
        We ask for one thing, the same thing the whole site runs on: tell us
        <strong>where you found it</strong>. A book with its author and page, or a
        link, is enough. That is what lets a maintainer check the claim, credit
        the source, and put it in front of the next reader with the same
        confidence as everything else here.
      </p>

      <p class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href={correctionEmail()}>
          Email a correction
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={newEntryEmail()}>
          Suggest an entry
        </a>
      </p>
      <p class="muted-note">
        Both open your mail app, addressed to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>
        with a few prompts already written. If you would rather just write in your
        own words, that address works on its own. Reading a specific entry? The
        <em>“Suggest a correction”</em> link on its catalog card does the same
        thing with the entry already filled in.
      </p>

      <h3>If you use GitHub</h3>
      <p>
        The whole site is public, and the history lives as plain Markdown files
        you can read and edit directly. Issues and pull requests are welcome, and
        are the fastest route if you are comfortable with them.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn" href={reportUrl()} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Open an issue
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/${REPO_BRANCH}/src/content`} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Edit the files
        </a>
        <a class="btn" href={newEntryUrl()} rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank">
          Propose a new entry
        </a>
      </p>
      <p>
        Either way, a maintainer reads every suggestion before it is published,
        and everyone whose work lands here is credited below.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

      <h3>Why is everything sourced?</h3>
      <p>
        Because trust is the point. A history that cannot be checked is just a
        story. Requiring a citation for every claim keeps the site honest and lets
        readers follow any fact back to where it comes from.
      </p>

      <h3>How is the history divided?</h3>
      <p>
        Into six eras, from Guru Nanak in 1469 to the present day. The framing is
        meant to be clear and faithful to the tradition; if you think an era is
        drawn wrongly, that is exactly the kind of feedback we welcome.
      </p>

      <h3>Who is behind it?</h3>
      <p>
        It is a volunteer, non-profit effort, dedicated to Waheguru and offered as
        a free resource for study, practice and research.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I reuse the content?</h3>
      <p>
        The site is intended as a free educational resource. Full licensing
        details will be published here.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contributors">Contributors</h2>
      <p>
        This is a volunteer effort. Everyone whose work is recorded in the
        project's history is credited here.
      </p>
      {
        contributors.length > 0 ? (
          <ul class="contributors">
            {contributors.map((c) => (
              <li>
                <span class="contributor-name">{c.name}</span>
                <span class="contributor-count">{c.count} {c.count === 1 ? 'edit' : 'edits'}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        ) : (
          <p class="muted-note">Contributor list will appear here once the site's history is published.</p>
        )
      }
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
